# ════════════════════════════════════════════════
# FILE: public/_redirects
# ════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# WHAT THIS DOES (plain language):
#   Tells Cloudflare which web addresses belong to the app. Every address listed
#   below is handed the app's home page, which then shows the right screen. An
#   address NOT listed gets a real "404 not found".
#
#   That second half is the whole point, and it is what closes the hazard that
#   took the site down twice on 2026-07-27.
#
# ════════════════════════════════════════════════
# THE HAZARD, AND WHAT WAS ACTUALLY CAUSING IT
# ════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# Symptom (twice on 2026-07-27): a request landed during a deployment for a file
# that was not there yet. Instead of failing, it received the app's HTML page
# with status "200 OK". `_headers` marks /app-assets/* cacheable for a YEAR, so
# Cloudflare and every device that touched that edge node kept the wrong answer.
# First time it hit a .js file and the app was a blank white screen; second time
# it hit a .css file and every page rendered unstyled.
#
# ⚠️ THE ORIGINAL DIAGNOSIS IN THIS FILE WAS WRONG, and that is why two attempts
# to fix it here failed. This file used to blame its own last line, the catch-all
#
#     /* /index.html 200
#
# It was never the cause. That rule was INERT — Cloudflare rejects it outright.
# Measured 2026-07-27 with `wrangler pages dev dist`, which reported
# "Parsed 0 valid redirect rules" and, for that exact line:
#
#     Infinite loop detected in this rule and has been ignored.
#
# Deleting the line changed nothing: a missing asset still returned 200 + HTML,
# and deep links still worked. Both facts had the same single explanation —
# Cloudflare Pages has a BUILT-IN fallback that serves index.html at 200 for any
# address it cannot find. Deep links were working by accident, and the poisoning
# came from that built-in behaviour, not from any rule in this file.
#
# So the earlier attempts were competing with something they could not reach:
#     /app-assets/* /asset-missing.html 404  -> ignored (404 is not a status
#                                               `_redirects` supports at all)
#     /app-assets/* /asset-missing     404   -> ignored, same reason
# A third variant was tried on 2026-07-27 and is WORSE — never ship it:
#     /app-assets/* /asset-not-found   200   -> parses as VALID, and rewrites
#                                               REAL assets to HTML too. Rewrites
#                                               here run BEFORE the file lookup,
#                                               so any rule matching /app-assets/*
#                                               breaks every asset it matches.
#
# ════════════════════════════════════════════════
# WHAT ACTUALLY CLOSES IT
# ════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# The built-in "200 + index.html for anything missing" fallback is disabled the
# moment a 404.html exists in the output directory. `public/404.html` is that
# file, and it is load-bearing — deleting it silently restores the outage.
#
# With the fallback gone, nothing serves the app any more, so every real address
# has to be listed here. Hence the rules below. A missing asset now matches no
# rule, finds no file, and returns 404 — verified below.
#
# Two rules per prefix because `/x/*` does not match a bare `/x`.
# The target is `/` and NOT `/index.html`, for two measured reasons:
#   - a rule with a `*` whose target ends in `.html` is rejected as an infinite
#     loop, exactly like the old catch-all;
#   - a rule WITHOUT a `*` is accepted but then 308-REDIRECTS to `/`, which
#     throws the address away — `/jobs` landed the user on the dashboard.
# `/ 200` is an internal rewrite: the app loads and the address is preserved.
#
# ⚠️ ADDING A ROUTE TO src/App.jsx NOW REQUIRES A LINE HERE. Without one, that
# page's address returns 404.html instead of the app.
# tests/qa/unit/spa-route-coverage.test.js re-derives this list from App.jsx and
# fails when one is missing, so this cannot drift silently.
#
# The list is deliberately a SUPERSET: it is generated from every route path
# segment, including ones nested under a parent (`/overview` lives under `/crm`).
# Listing an address that is not a real top-level route costs nothing — the app
# renders its own not-found screen, exactly as it does today — whereas MISSING
# one breaks a real page. Over-inclusion is the safe direction.
#
# Do not re-add a variant of the failed rules above without verifying it on a
# preview build first. A rule that looks protective but is inert is worse than
# none. How to verify, all four properties at once:
#   curl -sI <preview>/app-assets/does-not-exist.css   # want 404, not 200+HTML
#   curl -sI <preview>/app-assets/<a real hashed file>  # want 200 + immutable
#   curl -sI <preview>/crm/leads                        # want 200 + text/html
#   node scripts/smoke-deploy.mjs <preview>             # asserts all of the above
# ════════════════════════════════════════════════

